}\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs20 P?S/8 Binary File Internal Description\par
\par
Last edit:\tab 14-Nov-2017\tab cjl\par
\par
P?S/8 supports binary files in a variety of contexts, primarily as input to the multi-purpose binary loader program known as P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 .\par
\par
The binary file format is known as \i slurp\i0  binary; this term was coined by its inventor, the legendary Richard Lary, generally known as the best PDP-8 programmer in the history of mankind.  The term is an attempt to explain how the loader works without requiring a buffer, even when loading from certain storage devices traditionally associated with block-oriented access.  In\par
P?S/8, each system device generally has a companion slurp loader which is used by the BIN utility to load binary files on any particular system.  This is a customized routine geared to the same device as the system device handler for the express purpose of loading binary files.\par
\par
Note: The P?S/8 BIN \strike progtam \cf2\strike0 program \cf1 should not be confused with the name of standard binary paper-tapes which are generally loaded by the BIN loader.  That said, P/S/8 BIN does support certain operating modes that handle BIN (and RIM) binary paper-tapes, including the conversion of BIN format paper-tapes from/to P?S/8 slurp-format binary files.  When the terms \i BIN\i0  and \i file\i0  are used in the same sentence, this refers to slurp format binary files.\line\line The advantages of the custom loader include:\line\line 1) No reserved memory areas beyond the prevailing system convention (which is\par
   generally locations 00000-07577 unless an extended-memory device handler\par
   extension is required).\cf2   You mean no area other than 07600-07777, right?  Your sentence could be misunderstood as saying that 00000-07577 is reserved, though that obviously doesn't make sense.  Unless I'm completely confused here....   Migraine :( ....\cf1\par
\par
2) The ability to load memory with the system device write-protected (where\par
   applicable).\par
\par
3) Surprisingly rapid program loading which is often counter to the\par
   expectations of programmers taught to believe \cf2 that \cf1 what the slurp loader\par
   achieves is deemed impossible.\par
\par
The best example of a slurp binary loader implementation is the one customized for the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller.\par
\par
The TC01/TC08 system device handler loads into 07600-07777 and provides a read or write subroutine call to access the various logical drive units (either TU55 or TU56).  Certain locations are reserved for various system parameters and passed file data used by various system programs.  The operating system can always be restarted at location 07600 which is the universal starting address for all PDP-8 operating systems.\cf2   Are these "certain (memory) locations" in the 07600-07777 range too?  Now my dumbest question (in this paragraph, I think -- when an OS restarts @ 7600, it is reloaded by a loader located there?  \par
\par
I forget the details, but to my forgetful mind it seems that since the TC01/08 device handler is now in 07600-07777, something at 07600 is needed to reload the OS from some sort of media.  Or since we're talking DECtapes right here, do you simply load an OS tape and give the appropriate restart command and let it overwrite the device handler?  Damn!  Use it or lose it....  This paragraph's "confusion" is not related to this document!  Oh.... I should read teh next paragraph -- face palm time....\cf1\line\line Typical usage of the BIN program is to replace the contents of 07600-07777 with the slurp binary loader and a stand-alone bootstrap starting at 07600 which can also reload the operating system.  While the slurp loader is in memory, no read/write services are available.\par
\par
The slurp loader directly searches the DECtape logical drive units using passed file parameters of the starting block and logical unit for all input files passed to the BIN program.  When the proper starting block is located, the program is directly loaded into memory using the novel technique of using a one-word buffer within the slurp loader.\cf2   The slurp loader applies to other than just DECtapes, so should you state that it "... searches the ___ device logical drive units..."?  I forget the term that ought to be in the "___".\cf1\line\line The reason this is possible is that 12-bit words are brought into memory by the three-cycle data break mechanism; it is possible to detect individual word transfers by monitoring the associated word count (and/or current address) locations for an instant change.  Once the word count changes, it \cf2 i\cf1 s reset for the next transfer and the latest word transferred can then be used to effect the latest binary operation (which is primarily to load the latest word into memory and increment the location counter).\cf2   I have forgotten a lot of my English classes, but shouldn't "effect" above be "affect"?  Affect is a verb, effect is a noun.  <{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/affect-vs-effect/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 https://www.grammarly.com/blog/affect-vs-effect/}}}\f0\fs20 >  How do you interpret what they are saying?\cf1\page The slurp loader internal operations are analogous to those of the paper-tape binary loaders which use a completely different data format on paper-tape media.  All needed operations are provided in either loader; however, paper-tape loading is far slower and designed for that slow medium.  Slurp loading is nearly as efficient as block image loading, yet is random access loading much like paper-tape loading.\par
\par
In the case of the TC01/TC08 slurp loader, it is necessary for each word transfer to be completed nominally in 133 microseconds; this is easily achieved since the loader programming is short and efficient.\cf2   Is this the same for other storage devices?  133 \f1\u956?\f2 sec\f0  sounds awfully familiar, but I can't remember the details -- is this particular to DECtape?  Or does it apply to all devices because it is particular to the timing of the PDP-8* itself?  Maybe the 3-cycle data break requires the 133 \f1\u956?\f2 sec\f0  transfer?\cf1\par
\par
The loading process continues while the DECtape read operation spans multiple blocks; tape motion is continuously forward without turn-around.  Once the slurp end-of-file indication is processed, the next passed file parameters are processed, generally requiring a new block search.  Eventually all of the passed file parameters are processed and the loading process is complete.  At this point, a starting field and starting address are used to start the program as intended.  (This information was provided by initialization code in the BIN program as determined by command-line parameters provided by the user.  Additional command-line options may have been processed prior to moving the slurp loader through memory over the system device handler.)\cf2   What if the user doesn't know the starting field & starting address (like it is an unknown program, written by someone else)?  Does he/she have to guess at something like field 0 & address 200?  Or does he/she have to look at memory to try to find this information?\cf1\par
\par
On all releases of P?S/8, there is an alternative binary loader that uses virtual techniques to load binary files; however, due to the virtualization overhead, the loading process will tend to take more time.  Additionally, write enable is required on the logical device unit where the BIN program resides as the BIN program includes its own virtual buffer.\line\line While the majority of P?S/8 releases include a slurp loader customized to the same hardware as the system device handler, a few system configurations force the use of the virtual loader:\line\line 1) The RF08 is the only three-cycle data break device that cannot support a\par
   slurp loader; this is due to the transfer speed which doesn't provide\par
   sufficient time between word transfers.\par
\par
2) The RK8E and all other single-cycle data break (DMA) devices.  No method is\par
   provided to detect word transfers; the flag raises only at the end of block\par
   transfers.\cf2   Which devices are single-cycle DMA devices, therefore requiring virtual slurp loaders?\cf1\par
\par
All other devices are generally program-transfer devices which readily allow a device-specific slurp loader.\line\line An advantage of the virtual loader is that the system device handler is still resident after the program is loaded; however, all customized slurp loaders support optional reloading of the system device handler (which admittedly appears at first glance to be both counter-intuitive and impossible).  The only restriction on this usage is lowering the normal maximum file load count of 17 to 16 total input files; this is indicated by invoking the BIN command with the \cf3\highlight4\f3\fs24 /I\cf1\highlight0\f0\fs20  command-line switch.\par
\line While there are many additional details regarding loading options, the reader is directed to documentation of the BIN program for more information; the primary purpose of this document is to describe details of the slurp file format.\line\line Slurp format can be broken down into a series of contiguous file blocks, each independent of other blocks with the exception of the prevailing loading address and memory field.\line\line Each block of slurp format binary consists of a series of seven-word groups in contiguous order.  Except for the end-of-file block, there are always 18 seven-word groups in any block; each block also contains two waste words (since the blocks are logically 128 words each).\page Note: DECtape blocks are actually 129 12-bit words each; however, the 129th word is ignored throughout P?S/8.  On the TC01/TC08 system, the word count is generally set to -128 as required; as such, the 129th word is never transferred.  The majority of PDP-8 operating systems are designed to operate in a like manner.\cf2   Is this related to DECtape preceding the PDP-8 on older machines that weren't 12-bit?  I know you explained this at least once before, but other than a "memory" of the content of my previous sentence, I don't recall exactly what you said.  A "yes" or "no" answer is sufficient here so you won't have to rewrite a short "War and Peace" rexplaining the details of this comment's topic.\cf1\par
\par
Each seven-word group breaks down as follows:\line\line 1) A control word consisting of six two-bit status bits.\par
\par
2) The first of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[0-1] of the control word.\par
\par
3) The second of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[2-3] of the control word.\par
\par
4) The third of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[4-5] of the control word.\par
\par
5) The fourth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[6-7] of the control word.\par
\par
6) The fifth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[8-9] of the control word.\par
\par
7) The sixth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[10-11] of the control word.\par
\par
 Each pair of control word bits breaks down as follows:\line\line 1) 00 - The corresponding word is a data word; load this word into the memory\par
   location pointed to by the internal load pointer then increment the load\par
   pointer for the next data operation.  The loading field value in effect\par
   determines the memory field used for data loading.\cf2   The loading field value, in effect, determines..."?  "Loading field" is the field where the data is being loaded?  My first knee-jerk reaction is that a "loading field" is the field from which something is being loaded, but I think my knee-jerk interpretation is incorrect and the loading field _IS_ the field from where the data is being loaded.  I guess my initial confusion is the "...ing" on the end of many of the above words....  But, I believe that I am now interpreting the paragraph correctly.  Others that are also new to the format of the slurp loader may have the same incorrect knee-jerk interpretation when they read this paragraph, but if they think about it, they ought to correct themselves.  I hope I'm correct with my interpretation here!\cf1\par
\par
2) 01 - The end-of-file has been reached.  The slurp loader has finished\par
   processing this file; loading continues with the contents of the next\par
   passed file (if any).\par
\par
3) 10 - The corresponding word is an origin setting. \strike set \cf2\strike0 Set \cf1 the load pointer to\par
   the contents of this word.\par
\par
4) 11 - The corresponding word is a field setting in the form of CDF \i nn\i0  where\par
   nn is the new loading field in bits\cf2  \cf1 [6-8].\cf2   I added a space between "bits" and "[6-8]" here.\cf1\par
\par
Despite the fact that slurp format is inherently random access (and not block-oriented), the efficiency of data loading approaches 6/7 of that of core-image loading.  (P?S/8 system programs are stored in core-image format for the fastest possible loading speed; however, core-image data can only be loaded by accessing the system device handler.)\cf2   After looking at the slurp loader format details, now I understand the 6/7 efficiency!\cf1\line\line Practical implementations of programs that create slurp format files include repeatable characteristics within the file.  This allows other utilities to determine if a file may be in slurp binary format.  \cf2 "...to determine whether a file is in slurp binary..."?  \cf1 Additionally, multiple files may be loaded in the same overall loading operation.  Specific safeguards must be present to prevent potential loading mishap.\cf2   Where are these "specific safeguards" documented?\cf1\par
\par
